        'Take a concrete situation. You are developing an invoicing app in Next.js (TypeScript, Prisma, Stripe, next-auth, Vitest, Playwright) and you use Codex. You care about code quality, avoiding regressions and keeping agreements between sessions, but you don’t want a process that runs a dozen test suites after every small change. This is exactly the `examples/nextjs-codex` example in the kit’s repository, so you can reproduce every step.',
        'Step one is `npx github:RolePlayingTech/agent-harness-kit scan`. The scan finds the `typecheck` and `lint` scripts (the fast check), `test` (the full one), `test:e2e` (Playwright), the `prisma/migrations` directory and the `stripe` and `next-auth` dependencies. All commands are marked as detected in `package.json`, so there is no need to ask about them. Step two is `design`: in the terminal you answer a few questions, and Enter accepts the value the scan proposed. For a reproducible run the example ships `answers.json` with those answers (`design --yes --answers answers.json`). Your sentence translates into: the Codex tool, standard rigor (not strict, since you don’t want a heavy process), decisions memory (a decision log, without a handoff note), and two project rules.',
